page('Corrections needed in the mathematical narrative')
h('1. Lin can have stable positive balance')
p('For positive a and M=b+d+g+h-c, the synchronous value is x*=a/M. At that equilibrium, with theta_j=2*pi*j/5, the eigenvalue real parts are:')
p('Re(lambda_j) = x*[-b + (c-g)cos(theta_j)<br/>                         - (d+h)cos(2 theta_j)].','EqX')
p('This reproduces the baseline eigenvalues. The concrete counterexample (a,b,c,d,g,h)=(1,2,1,1,1,1) has x*=0.25 and largest real part -0.095492. Thus the statement that Lin cannot represent stable balance is false. Use it as a one-sentence qualification, without turning the section into a new theorem paper.')
h('2. Local stability, boundedness and equilibrium existence differ')
p('Strictly negative eigenvalue real parts imply local exponential stability. A positive real part implies instability. A zero real part requires further analysis. Bounded trajectories need not approach an equilibrium. Convergence from several starts is not global stability, and failure to find a positive synchronous equilibrium does not make an ODE ill posed.')
p('For Lin on the synchronous subspace, s\'=a*s-M*s^2. With a&gt;0 and positive s(0), M&lt;0 gives finite-time blow-up; M=0 gives exponential growth, not finite-time blow-up; M&gt;0 gives convergence to a/M on this subspace. Do not extend the last conclusion to every nonsynchronous trajectory without a separate argument.')
p('The record\'s c=3.9 symmetric stress test reports a maximum near 131 from s(0)=1, despite M=0.1. The exact scalar trajectory increases monotonically to 10. That excursion cannot be a feature of the exact symmetric solution; numerical loss of symmetry must be investigated before using it as evidence.')
h('3. The simple modified model has a valid boundedness argument')
p('Assume nonnegative coefficients and k&gt;0. At x_i=0 its derivative is c*x_(i-1), which is nonnegative. At a component attaining the maximum X, the derivative is at most a*X-k*X^2+c*X/(1+X), and hence at most a*X-k*X^2+c. Therefore the box [0,B]^5 is positively invariant and attracts from above, where:')
p('B = [a + sqrt(a^2 + 4*k*c)]/(2*k).','EqX')
p('For arbitrary nonnegative initial data, max_i x_i(t) is bounded by max{max_i x_i(0),B}. This is a boundedness statement for the simple model, not a convergence proof and not automatically a result for every extension.')
h('4. Describe what actually changed between the models')
p('The simple modified model omits the direct CHILD loss, changes the nourishment law, and changes the target dependence. In Lin, the target derivative is -h*x_i; in the modified model it is +g*x_i/(1+x_target)^2. The target interaction therefore changes sign. The nourishment c*x_mother/(1+x_i) is linear in the mother and decreases with recipient activity; it is not a conventional saturation in the mother variable. Avoid saying the models have identical interactions differing only by saturation.')
h('5. Parameter windows are assumed scenarios')
p('The proposed ordering and tiered ranges are not fitted or physiologically established. Raw coefficients multiplying different functional forms are not automatically comparable in strength; scaling, units and activity level matter. Describe these as sensitivity scenarios, and report them explicitly.')

